window.SEARCH=[{"t":"What makes a robot mobile, and why that changes everything","c":"Chapter 1","u":"ch1.html#s1","x":"A stationary robot (an industrial arm bolted to a floor) works inside a fixed, known, fenced workspace. Its base frame never moves, so every joint angle maps to exactly one tool position, and its surroundings are engineered to be predictable. A mobile robot carries its own base through the world. It must answer three questions continuously: where am I , where am I going and how do I get there , an"},{"t":"Intelligence needs a body: the embodied view of autonomy","c":"Chapter 1","u":"ch1.html#s2","x":"Early AI treated intelligence as symbol manipulation: plan in an abstract model, then execute. Mobile robots exposed the weakness of this view. A plan computed on a perfect map fails the moment a wheel slips or a person steps into the corridor. Embodiment is the idea that intelligent behaviour emerges from a closed loop between a physical body, its sensors and actuators, and the world. The robot's"},{"t":"A roboticist's problem: turning a task into sensing, estimation and control","c":"Chapter 1","u":"ch1.html#s3","x":"Correll frames the roboticist's problem as follows. Given a task (deliver a tote to station 4), a body and a set of sensors, design the software that closes the loop reliably. The task decomposes into four sub-problems that map directly onto this course: Locomotion and kinematics : how commanded wheel speeds become motion (Chapter 1, Chapter 4). Perception : how raw sensor data relate to the world"},{"t":"The fundamental requirements every mobile robot must meet","c":"Chapter 1","u":"ch1.html#s4","x":"TLO3 asks you to discuss the fundamental requirements of a mobile robot. Examiners expect these, each with a one-line justification: Locomotion : a mechanism matched to the terrain (wheels on floors, tracks on soil, legs on stairs). Perception : proprioceptive sensors (encoders, IMU, battery monitor) measure the robot itself; exteroceptive sensors (LiDAR, camera, ultrasonic, bump) measure the worl"},{"t":"Why mobile autonomy is hard: the five challenges","c":"Chapter 1","u":"ch1.html#s5","x":"TLO4 asks you to list and explain the challenges. Write them as follows: Sensor uncertainty : every sensor has noise, limited range and resolution, and can be fooled (glass walls to LiDAR, sunlight to infrared, symmetric corridors to any sensor). Actuation uncertainty : wheels slip, carpets compress, payloads shift the centre of mass; the commanded motion is never exactly executed. Unknown and dyn"},{"t":"Which industries did robots transform, and in what order","c":"Chapter 1","u":"ch1.html#s6","x":"Review question 5 asks which industries robots entered first and which were revolutionized recently. Robots were introduced first in automotive manufacturing : the Unimate arm joined General Motors' die-casting line in 1961, followed by spot welding and painting in the 1970s and electronics assembly in the 1980s. These were all stationary robots in structured cells. The recent revolutions are driv"},{"t":"Locomotion: the ways a robot can move itself","c":"Chapter 1","u":"ch1.html#s7","x":"Locomotion is the process by which a robot moves its own body through the environment; it is distinct from manipulation , which moves objects. The choice of locomotion fixes the robot's mobility, efficiency and terrain capability. Mobility is the robot's ability to move in its environment; formally, the set of velocities it can achieve from a given configuration. A differential-drive robot has lim"},{"t":"Static versus dynamic stability","c":"Chapter 1","u":"ch1.html#s8","x":"A robot is statically stable if it remains at rest, without any control effort, when its motors are switched off. The condition is that the vertical projection of the centre of mass (CoM) lies inside the support polygon , the convex hull of the ground contact points. A robot is dynamically stable if it stays upright only through continuous active control, as in balancing or running. Review questio"},{"t":"Degrees of freedom, controllable DoF and holonomy","c":"Chapter 1","u":"ch1.html#s9","x":"The degrees of freedom (DoF) of a rigid body are the independent coordinates needed to describe its configuration: 6 in 3D space, 3 in the plane . For wheeled robots we distinguish: DoF of the configuration : the dimension of the space of poses the robot can reach, normally 3 for a planar robot. Differentiable or controllable DoF (DDoF) : the number of independent velocities the robot can command "},{"t":"Wheel types determine the constraints","c":"Chapter 1","u":"ch1.html#s10","x":"Counting DoF for a wheeled robot is therefore a matter of counting independent sliding constraints. Each standard wheel on a common axle forbids the same sideways direction, so several wheels on one axle give only one independent constraint. Castors add support without adding constraints. Do not count the wheel's own spin or the castor's swivel as degrees of freedom of the robot. The question is a"},{"t":"Worked DoF answers: differential robot, office chair, car","c":"Chapter 1","u":"ch1.html#s11","x":"Differential-drive robot with one front castor. The chassis moves in the plane, so DoF = 3 . The two drive wheels share one axle and impose one sideways-slip constraint; the castor imposes none. Controllable DoF = 3 - 1 = 2 (forward speed and turn rate ). It is nonholonomic, yet it can reach any pose by combining the two. Adding a second castor changes nothing kinematically: DoF stays 3, DDoF stay"},{"t":"Why every robot quantity needs a frame","c":"Chapter 1","u":"ch1.html#s12","x":"A LiDAR reports that an obstacle is 2 m ahead. Ahead of what? The number is meaningful only in the sensor frame . To avoid the obstacle the planner needs it in the robot frame ; to put it in a map it must be expressed in the world (map) frame . A frame of reference is an origin plus a set of orthonormal basis vectors; a coordinate system assigns numbers to points relative to that frame. ROS formal"},{"t":"Rotation matrices: entries are dot products of basis vectors","c":"Chapter 1","u":"ch1.html#s13","x":"Let frame {B} share its origin with frame {A}. The rotation matrix takes coordinates expressed in {B} and returns them in {A}. Its columns are the basis vectors of {B} written in {A}; equivalently each entry is a dot product (a direction cosine): This is the answer to ISA-1 question 1a. The matrix is orthonormal, so its inverse is its transpose, , and its determinant is +1."},{"t":"Deriving the rotation about z and verifying the dot-product rule","c":"Chapter 1","u":"ch1.html#s14","x":"ISA-1 question 2c asks for this derivation. Take {B} rotated by angle about . In {A} coordinates, , and . Placing these as columns gives Verification entry by entry: ; (the y-axis of {B} tilts towards negative ); ; ; every product with is 0 except . Each entry is therefore the scalar product of a basis vector of one frame with a basis vector of the other, as required. Always state the direction co"},{"t":"Homogeneous transforms combine rotation and translation","c":"Chapter 1","u":"ch1.html#s15","x":"If {B} is also displaced so that its origin sits at in {A}, the frame is written . A point transforms as . Packing both into one 4 x 4 (or 3 x 3 in the plane) matrix lets chains of frames be multiplied: This answers review question 6 and ISA-1 question 1b. For the inverse (ISA-1 question 2b, with {B} and {C}), use orthonormality rather than a general matrix inverse:"},{"t":"Worked example: a point through a planar transform and back","c":"Chapter 1","u":"ch1.html#s16","x":"Frame {B} is rotated 30 degrees relative to {A} and its origin is at m. A LiDAR in {B} sees a point at m. Find , then invert. . m. m. Inverse translation: m. Check: m, the original point."},{"t":"Forward kinematics of a differential-drive robot","c":"Chapter 1","u":"ch1.html#s17","x":"Forward kinematics maps actuator velocities to the chassis velocity. For a differential drive with wheel radius , wheel separation and wheel speeds , each wheel's ground speed is . Both wheels rotate about a common instantaneous centre of rotation (ICR) on the wheel axis: In the world frame the pose then evolves as . Equal wheel speeds give a straight line (); equal and opposite speeds turn on the"},{"t":"Worked example: forward kinematics over two seconds","c":"Chapter 1","u":"ch1.html#s18","x":"m, m, rad/s, rad/s, starting at for s. m/s. rad/s, so m. Heading after 2 s: rad = 76.4 degrees. On a circle about : m, m. Check: the arc length m equals . This closed-form arc is exactly the noise-free velocity motion model of Chapter 4."},{"t":"Inverse kinematics: from desired motion to wheel speeds","c":"Chapter 1","u":"ch1.html#s19","x":"Inverse kinematics asks the opposite question: which wheel speeds produce a desired ? Inverting the two linear equations gives With the robot above, m/s and rad/s need rad/s and rad/s. This is the computation inside every ROS diff_drive_controller when it receives a cmd_vel message. The hard version of inverse kinematics is at the pose level: which wheel-speed history takes the robot to a goal pos"},{"t":"Inverse kinematics using feedback control","c":"Chapter 1","u":"ch1.html#s20","x":"Express the error to the goal in polar coordinates: distance , the angle between the heading and the line to the goal, and the angle between that line and the goal heading: The control law drives all three errors to zero. The closed loop is locally exponentially stable when and (Siegwart and Nourbakhsh)."},{"t":"Worked example and live controller","c":"Chapter 1","u":"ch1.html#s21","x":"Robot at m heading 90 degrees; goal m heading 0; gains . , so m. . . m/s; rad/s (turn right towards the goal). Wheel speeds then follow from the inverse kinematics of the previous slide. Real controllers saturate and ; the proportional law asks for high speed far from the goal. The polar form is singular at , so implementations stop or switch to a pure heading controller inside a small radius. If "},{"t":"Path planning needs a map, and the map decides the algorithm","c":"Chapter 2","u":"ch2.html#s1","x":"Path planning finds a collision-free sequence of configurations from a start to a goal, usually minimising a cost such as length, time or risk. Before any algorithm can run, the free space must be represented. Three representations dominate: Criterion Occupancy grid Topological graph Continuous (polygons) Built from Range sensors directly (Ch 7) Places and connections, by hand or segmentation CAD "},{"t":"Configuration space: planning for a point instead of a robot","c":"Chapter 2","u":"ch2.html#s2","x":"Planning for a robot of finite size is simplified by the configuration space (C-space) : the space of all robot poses. Each obstacle is grown by the robot's shape to form a C-space obstacle; the robot then becomes a point. For a circular robot of radius the growth is simply a dilation of every obstacle by , which is what the inflation layer of a ROS costmap does. For a rectangular robot the C-spac"},{"t":"Dijkstra: expand in order of cost-to-come","c":"Chapter 2","u":"ch2.html#s3","x":"On a grid treated as a graph (cells are nodes, neighbours are edges with cost 1 or ), Dijkstra's algorithm keeps a priority queue ordered by the cost-to-come . It repeatedly removes the cheapest node, marks it closed and relaxes its neighbours: if , set and the parent pointer. When the goal is removed from the queue, following parents backwards gives the optimal path. Initialise , all others infin"},{"t":"A*: add a heuristic that points towards the goal","c":"Chapter 2","u":"ch2.html#s4","x":"A* orders the queue by , where estimates the remaining cost. If never overestimates (it is admissible ), A* still returns the optimal path, and if is consistent each node is expanded at most once. On an 8-connected grid the Euclidean or octile distance is admissible. Setting recovers Dijkstra; inflating it (, , weighted A*) expands fewer cells but gives paths up to times the optimum. Draw a wall t"},{"t":"D*: replanning when the map changes","c":"Chapter 2","u":"ch2.html#s5","x":"A robot discovers obstacles as it moves. Replanning from scratch with A* after every change is wasteful because most of the previous search is still valid. D* (Stentz, 1994) and its simpler successor D* Lite (Koenig and Likhachev, 2002) search backwards from the goal and, when edge costs change, repair only the affected part of the search tree. On Mars, the rover navigation software of NASA's rove"},{"t":"Why sample: grids explode in high dimensions","c":"Chapter 2","u":"ch2.html#s6","x":"A grid with 100 cells per axis has cells in 2D, for , and for a 6-joint arm. Sampling-based planners avoid building this grid: they draw random configurations, keep the collision-free ones and connect them with short local motions. They are probabilistically complete : if a path exists, the probability of finding it tends to 1 as samples grow. PRM (probabilistic roadmap) : sample many configuratio"},{"t":"RRT in action","c":"Chapter 2","u":"ch2.html#s7","x":"Switch between RRT and RRT* with the same number of samples. RRT* paths straighten as samples increase; plain RRT paths do not."},{"t":"Runtime: nearest-neighbour search dominates","c":"Chapter 2","u":"ch2.html#s8","x":"Review question 5: every RRT iteration must find the nearest existing node. A linear scan costs , so building a tree of nodes costs . With a kd-tree and uniformly random samples, the tree stays approximately balanced, so both inserting a point and querying the nearest neighbour take expected time (its depth). Building the whole tree is then . The worst case of a single insertion into a badly unbal"},{"t":"Path smoothing: the robot cannot drive a staircase","c":"Chapter 2","u":"ch2.html#s9","x":"Grid and RRT paths have sharp corners that a wheeled robot cannot follow at speed. A simple and widely taught smoother moves each interior waypoint to balance two goals: stay near the original waypoint and stay near its neighbours: iterated until the change is small, with start and goal fixed. A large straightens the path; a large keeps it close to the collision-checked original. Smoothing can pul"},{"t":"One planner cannot do everything","c":"Chapter 2","u":"ch2.html#s10","x":"A delivery robot in a hospital must choose which lift to take (hundreds of metres, decided rarely), plan a corridor path (tens of metres, replanned when blocked), dodge a trolley (a few metres, tens of times per second) and hold wheel speeds (centimetres, hundreds of times per second). No single algorithm is both globally optimal and fast enough for the last layer, so planning is hierarchical . In"},{"t":"Variations of the path-planning problem","c":"Chapter 2","u":"ch2.html#s11","x":"Review question 3 asks you to discuss variations of the planning problem. The core problem assumes a known, static map, a point robot and a single goal. Real systems relax each assumption: Variation What changes Typical method Unknown or partially known map Obstacles discovered while moving D* Lite, frontier exploration Dynamic obstacles Obstacles move; time becomes a dimension Velocity obstacles,"},{"t":"Where robot uncertainty comes from","c":"Chapter 2","u":"ch2.html#s12","x":"Review question 1 (and ESA question 1c) asks for the factors that lead to robot uncertainty. Thrun, Burgard and Fox list five; write each with an example: Environments are unpredictable: people walk, doors open, pallets are moved. Sensors are limited by physics (range, resolution, field of view) and corrupted by noise; a LiDAR cannot see through glass or beyond 30 m. Robot actuation is imprecise: "},{"t":"Error propagation: how uncertainty flows through a function","c":"Chapter 2","u":"ch2.html#s13","x":"If an input has covariance and , first-order error propagation linearises around the mean with its Jacobian : For dead reckoning with pose and noisy controls with covariance , the pose covariance evolves as where is the Jacobian with respect to the previous pose and the Jacobian with respect to the controls. This single equation is the prediction step of the extended Kalman filter in Chapter 3."},{"t":"Worked example and live error propagation","c":"Chapter 2","u":"ch2.html#s14","x":"A robot drives 5 m straight with a constant heading error of 2 degrees. Lateral offset m. If instead the heading error is random with standard deviation 2 degrees, the lateral standard deviation is approximately m, growing linearly with distance, while along-track error from speed noise grows only with the square root of time. Conclusion: gyro quality matters more than encoder quality for long str"},{"t":"The key idea: represent uncertainty explicitly with probability","c":"Chapter 2","u":"ch2.html#s15","x":"Probabilistic robotics replaces the single best guess with a probability distribution over all possible states, called the belief : the probability of state given all measurements and controls so far. Perception and action both become probabilistic models: a measurement model and a motion model . Everything in Chapters 3-7 is built from these two ingredients combined by Bayes' rule. Two advantages"},{"t":"Probabilistic robots in the field","c":"Chapter 2","u":"ch2.html#s16","x":"The RHINO (Bonn, 1997) and MINERVA (Smithsonian, 1998) tour-guide robots, built by the authors of the course textbook, used Markov and Monte Carlo localization to navigate crowded museums for thousands of hours. Stanley, Stanford's winner of the 2005 DARPA Grand Challenge, used probabilistic sensor fusion to classify terrain as drivable. Every modern AMR stack, including Nav2's AMCL node, descends"},{"t":"Random variables, densities and the Gaussian","c":"Chapter 2","u":"ch2.html#s17","x":"A random variable takes values with probabilities. Discrete variables have a probability mass summing to 1; continuous variables have a probability density function (pdf) integrating to 1. The most important pdf in robotics is the normal (Gaussian) distribution: is the mean and the variance; in the multivariate case is the symmetric positive-definite covariance matrix."},{"t":"Joint, conditional, total probability and independence","c":"Chapter 2","u":"ch2.html#s18","x":"The joint distribution gives the probability of both events. If and are independent , . The conditional probability is . The theorem of total probability marginalises a variable out: This is the prediction step of every filter: the new belief is the old belief pushed through the motion model and summed over all previous states. Conditional independence , , is what makes filters tractable: given th"},{"t":"Bayes' rule: from a sensor model to a state estimate","c":"Chapter 2","u":"ch2.html#s19","x":"Bayes' rule inverts a conditional. We know the sensor model (how likely a reading is in each state) and want : is the prior , the likelihood , the posterior , and a normaliser computed by summing the numerator over all . Prior probability a cell is occupied: 0.2. Sensor: , . Numerators: ; . ; posterior . Check: one reading raises belief from 0.2 to 0.69, not to 0.9; the prior matters."},{"t":"State: everything that matters for the future","c":"Chapter 3","u":"ch3.html#s1","x":"The state collects all aspects of the robot and its environment that can influence the future. Review question 1 asks for the typical state variables: Robot pose : in the plane, or six values in 3D. Robot velocities : , or wheel speeds, needed for dynamic models. Actuator configuration : joint angles of an on-board arm, gripper state. Locations and features of surrounding objects : landmarks, wall"},{"t":"Measurements, controls and their data streams","c":"Chapter 3","u":"ch3.html#s2","x":"ISA-1 question 2a asks for four terms. Write them precisely: Sensor measurements are the information the robot obtains about its environment through its sensors; they reveal state only indirectly and noisily. Control actions change the state of the world, most importantly the robot's own motion; even doing nothing lets the world change. Measurement data : is the measurement at time ; is the sequen"},{"t":"Probabilistic generative laws and the dynamic Bayes network","c":"Chapter 3","u":"ch3.html#s3","x":"Under the Markov assumption the evolution of state and measurements is governed by two conditional distributions: The belief after incorporating is ; the prediction before incorporating it is ."},{"t":"The Bayes filter algorithm","c":"Chapter 3","u":"ch3.html#s4","x":"The Bayes filter computes recursively from , the control and the measurement . For every state : Derivation sketch. Apply Bayes' rule to and use the measurement Markov assumption to reduce to ; this gives the correction. Then expand by total probability over and use the state Markov assumption to reduce the transition to ; the remaining factor is , since future controls carry no information about "},{"t":"The door example: a Bayes filter you can compute by hand","c":"Chapter 3","u":"ch3.html#s5","x":"Review question 2. A robot estimates whether a door is open. Sensor model: , . Actions: doing nothing leaves the door unchanged; pushing opens a closed door with probability 0.8 and never closes an open one. Initial belief 0.5 / 0.5. = do nothing: = (0.5, 0.5). = sense open: , so = (0.75, 0.25). = push: ; . = sense open: , so ."},{"t":"Try the door filter","c":"Chapter 3","u":"ch3.html#s6","x":"Press Sense closed five times from a belief of 0.983 open. How many contradicting readings does it take to change the robot's mind, and why is that the right behaviour for a noisy sensor?"},{"t":"Representation and computation: the Bayes filter is not an algorithm yet","c":"Chapter 3","u":"ch3.html#s7","x":"For a continuous state the prediction integral and the correction have no closed form in general, so the Bayes filter is a framework . A concrete filter must choose how to represent the belief and how to compute the update. ISA-1 question 3b asks about the discrete Bayes filter and its use in continuous spaces: in a finite state space the integral becomes a sum and the filter is exact; for continu"},{"t":"Why Gaussians: closed-form updates","c":"Chapter 3","u":"ch3.html#s8","x":"Gaussian filters represent the belief by a mean and covariance . The Gaussian family is closed under linear transformation and under multiplication by Gaussian likelihoods, so if the motion and measurement models are linear with additive Gaussian noise, the Bayes filter becomes exact matrix algebra. This is the moments parameterization ; the canonical parameterization with , gives the information "},{"t":"The Kalman filter algorithm","c":"Chapter 3","u":"ch3.html#s9","x":"Algorithm Kalman_filter : The first line is the prediction; the rest is the correction. is the Kalman gain : it weighs the innovation by how uncertain the prediction is relative to the measurement. If the measurement is precise () the gain approaches and the filter trusts the sensor; if the prediction is precise () the gain approaches 0 and the sensor is ignored."},{"t":"Deriving the Kalman filter","c":"Chapter 3","u":"ch3.html#s10","x":"Review question 4 and ISA-1 question 3c ask for this derivation. Outline with the key steps: Prediction. . A linear transform of a Gaussian plus independent Gaussian noise is Gaussian with mean and covariance . Correction. with . is quadratic in , so the belief is Gaussian. Setting gives the covariance; setting at gives . The matrix inversion lemma rewrites as and . In one dimension with : , , ."},{"t":"Illustrating the Kalman filter","c":"Chapter 3","u":"ch3.html#s11","x":"Review question 3 asks for illustrations of the Kalman filter with neat diagrams. The standard sequence is: prior and measurement combine into a sharper posterior; motion shifts and widens the belief; a second measurement sharpens it again. . . , so . The posterior lies between prior and measurement, nearer the more certain one, and is narrower than either."},{"t":"Simulating a mobile robot with a Kalman filter","c":"Chapter 3","u":"ch3.html#s12","x":"Review question 5 (TLO3) asks you to simulate a mobile robot estimating its present state. The tool below is a 1D cart moving at a commanded 1 m/s with process noise and a position sensor with noise , filtered once per second. For a 2D robot with constant-velocity state , has the form and a GPS-like sensor gives . Set to 3 m. The gain settles near a small value and the belief lags the true cart le"},{"t":"The extended Kalman filter: linearise, then apply Kalman","c":"Chapter 3","u":"ch3.html#s13","x":"Robot models are nonlinear: (the motion contains ) and (range and bearing involve square roots and atan2). The EKF linearises both by a first-order Taylor expansion at the current mean, with Jacobians and : Linearisation error grows with nonlinearity and with uncertainty. A large heading uncertainty makes strongly nonlinear over the belief, so the EKF can become overconfident and diverge. The unsc"},{"t":"The information filter: the Kalman filter in canonical form","c":"Chapter 3","u":"ch3.html#s14","x":"ISA-1 question 3a asks you to derive an information filter. Replace by the information matrix and information vector . Substituting into the Kalman equations gives: Derivation of the correction: the exponent of the Kalman derivation expands to ; reading off the quadratic and linear coefficients gives and directly. The correction is now just addition , which makes multi-sensor fusion and multi-robo"},{"t":"The histogram filter: decompose the state space","c":"Chapter 3","u":"ch3.html#s15","x":"The histogram filter divides the state space into finitely many regions and keeps one probability per region. The Bayes filter becomes the discrete Bayes filter : It can represent any multimodal belief, at the cost of resolution and of memory that grows exponentially with state dimension. Static decomposition uses a fixed grid; dynamic decomposition (density trees) refines bins where probability i"},{"t":"The particle filter: represent belief by samples","c":"Chapter 3","u":"ch3.html#s16","x":"The particle filter represents by a set of samples . Dense regions of particles mean high probability. Algorithm Particle_filter : For each particle : sample (prediction by sampling the motion model). Compute the importance weight (correction). Resample : draw particles with replacement, with probability proportional to . Resampling turns the weighted set into an unweighted sample of the posterior"},{"t":"Worked particle weights and practical issues","c":"Chapter 3","u":"ch3.html#s17","x":"Particles at m; measurement says m with m. Unnormalised weights : 0.006, 0.487, 0.726, 0.020. Normalised: 0.005, 0.393, 0.586, 0.016. Weighted mean 2.61 m; effective sample size : only two particles really count, so resampling is warranted. Particle deprivation: with too few particles none may lie near the true state, and the filter never recovers; add random particles (Chapter 6). Very accurate s"},{"t":"The pose: three numbers for a planar robot","c":"Chapter 4","u":"ch4.html#s1","x":"The kinematic configuration (or kinematic state) of a rigid mobile robot in a planar environment is its pose , the position and orientation relative to a global frame: Review question 2 asks you to illustrate this model. Six variables are needed in 3D (three Cartesian, three Euler angles), but a robot on a flat floor has only three. The location is ; the bearing or heading is . A pose with points "},{"t":"From deterministic to probabilistic kinematics","c":"Chapter 4","u":"ch4.html#s2","x":"Chapter 1 gave , a deterministic function. In reality the same command applied twice gives two different poses. Probabilistic kinematics replaces the function with the motion model the posterior over the successor pose given the previous pose and the control. It is the state-transition distribution of the Bayes filter (Chapter 3) and it is used in two forms: Density form : given , , , return the n"},{"t":"What probabilistic kinematics means","c":"Chapter 4","u":"ch4.html#s3","x":"Review question 1 asks you to explain probabilistic kinematics. The key statements: The motion of a real robot is subject to noise from wheel slip, uneven floors, tyre deformation, motor control errors and unmodelled dynamics. Instead of predicting one successor pose, we predict a probability distribution over successor poses. The model is stated for small time steps and planar rigid robots. It as"},{"t":"Algorithms for sampling from distributions","c":"Chapter 4","u":"ch4.html#s4","x":"Sampling motion models need random numbers with a specified variance. Two standard samplers for zero mean and variance : The sum of 12 uniform variables on has variance , so dividing by 6 and multiplying by gives variance ; by the central limit theorem it is close to Gaussian. The corresponding density function returns the zero-mean normal (or triangular) density of . Throughout this chapter, is a"},{"t":"What the uncertainty looks like: the banana","c":"Chapter 4","u":"ch4.html#s5","x":"If a robot starts from a known pose and executes a motion, the distribution of successor poses is not an ellipse. Heading error at the start of the motion is amplified into lateral error along the path, so the distribution bends into the characteristic banana shape. The effect grows with distance and with rotational noise. Warehouse AMRs drive long straight aisles. Odometry alone, typically accura"},{"t":"Problems and design choices","c":"Chapter 4","u":"ch4.html#s6","x":"Choosing and using a motion model raises recurring problems: Velocity or odometry? Odometry is usually more accurate because it measures what the wheels did rather than what was commanded, but it is available only after the motion, so it cannot be used for planning. Time step. Both models assume constant controls over ; long steps with changing speeds violate this. Tuning : too small and the filte"},{"t":"Controls as velocities, motion as arcs","c":"Chapter 4","u":"ch4.html#s7","x":"The velocity motion model assumes the robot is controlled through a translational velocity and a rotational velocity : . Differential-drive, synchro-drive and skid-steer robots are commonly commanded this way (a ROS cmd_vel message). If both are held constant for , the robot moves on a circle of radius about the centre and the noise-free successor pose is"},{"t":"Adding noise, and why a third parameter gamma is needed","c":"Chapter 4","u":"ch4.html#s8","x":"The executed velocities are the commanded ones plus zero-mean noise whose variance grows with the commands: govern translational error, rotational error. With only the successor pose would always lie exactly on some circle, so the final heading would be determined by the position: the distribution would live on a 2D surface inside 3D pose space, which is degenerate. The model therefore adds a fina"},{"t":"Algorithm sample_motion_model_velocity","c":"Chapter 4","u":"ch4.html#s9","x":"Review question 4 asks you to explain this algorithm (ISA-2 question 1c, 10 marks). Input: , . return The derivation (review question 5, ISA-2 question 3a) is exactly this: perturb the controls by their noise model, then apply the exact circular-arc kinematics of the previous slide to the perturbed controls. Calling it times on particles implements the prediction step of a particle filter. When is"},{"t":"Algorithm motion_model_velocity (density form)","c":"Chapter 4","u":"ch4.html#s10","x":"Given a hypothesised successor , the density form works backwards to the controls that would have produced it and scores their errors: (circle centre) return Line 1 finds the centre of the unique circle through both positions whose tangent at the start has heading ; the rest follows from arc geometry."},{"t":"Worked example: evaluating the velocity density","c":"Chapter 4","u":"ch4.html#s11","x":"Start ; command m/s, rad/s for s; hypothesis ; . . Centre , ; so m. rad. m/s, rad/s, rad/s. Variances: ; ; . Densities: , , ; product . The value is a density, so it can exceed 1; what matters is its relative size across hypotheses."},{"t":"Sampling the velocity model live","c":"Chapter 4","u":"ch4.html#s12","x":""},{"t":"Applications and limitations of the velocity model","c":"Chapter 4","u":"ch4.html#s13","x":"Review question 3 and ISA-2 question 1b ask for the applications of the velocity motion model: Prediction in filters when no odometry exists , for example legged robots, drones or simulated robots where only commands are known. Motion planning and control : because it uses commands, it can predict the outcome of a candidate command before executing it. The dynamic window approach and model-predict"},{"t":"Using odometry as a measurement of the control","c":"Chapter 4","u":"ch4.html#s14","x":"Most wheeled robots integrate wheel-encoder counts into an odometry pose estimate in their own internal frame, which drifts relative to the world. The odometry motion model uses only the relative motion between two successive odometry poses, , which is accurate over short intervals even though the absolute odometry drifts. The relative motion is decomposed into an initial rotation, a translation a"},{"t":"Algorithm sample_motion_model_odometry","c":"Chapter 4","u":"ch4.html#s15","x":"Review question 6 and ISA-2 question 3b ask you to discuss the odometry algorithm. Input: , current particle . Compute from the odometry as above. The noise parameters have clear meaning: rotation error from rotation, rotation error from translation, translation error from translation, translation error from rotation. The density form motion_model_odometry computes the same three deltas from the o"},{"t":"Worked example: decompose and evaluate","c":"Chapter 4","u":"ch4.html#s16","x":"Odometry reports . Hypothesis: the robot moved from to . . Odometry: ; m; . Hypothesis: ; m; . Density product , with variances computed from the hypothesis deltas as in the algorithm. A hypothesis further from the odometry, for example 30 cm off, would score orders of magnitude lower."},{"t":"Simulating odometry with locomotion and steering","c":"Chapter 4","u":"ch4.html#s17","x":"Review question 8 asks you to simulate the odometry motion model with locomotion and steering. Use the tool: the sliders set the odometry reading (the locomotion displacement and the steering change of heading) and the noise parameters; every redraw calls the sampler 500 times from the start pose. Set and (driving straight). The cloud is an ellipse elongated along x. Now add steering: the same noi"},{"t":"Velocity versus odometry motion model","c":"Chapter 4","u":"ch4.html#s18","x":"TLO2 asks you to distinguish the two models. Use this table in an answer: Criterion Velocity motion model Odometry motion model Control Commanded Pair of odometry poses Available Before motion: usable for planning After motion only: estimation only Accuracy Lower: commands differ from execution Higher: measures executed wheel motion Motion primitive Circular arc per step Rotate, translate, rotate "},{"t":"The measurement model needs a map","c":"Chapter 5","u":"ch5.html#s1","x":"The measurement model describes how sensor readings are generated from the robot pose and the environment map . A map is a list of objects in the environment and their properties, . Two kinds of index are used: Feature-based maps : is a feature index and holds the Cartesian location (and possibly a signature) of the feature. Compact, and natural for landmarks, poles, corners, fiducials. Location-b"},{"t":"Measurements are many values, assumed independent","c":"Chapter 5","u":"ch5.html#s2","x":"A modern sensor returns many values per reading: a 2D LiDAR gives = 360 to 2000 ranges, . The measurement model assumes the individual beams are conditionally independent given pose and map: This is an idealisation. Beams are correlated through people not in the map, map errors and approximations in the model itself. Its practical consequence is overconfidence: 1000 beams multiply 1000 slightly wr"},{"t":"Four sources of range error","c":"Chapter 5","u":"ch5.html#s3","x":"The beam model is a physical model of a single range beam of a laser or sonar. Given the pose and map, ray casting gives the true range to the nearest obstacle, . TLO3 asks you to explain the four types of measurement error a real reading can have: Correct range with local noise : limited resolution, atmospheric effects, surface reflectance. A Gaussian centred on with standard deviation , truncate"},{"t":"The mixture density","c":"Chapter 5","u":"ch5.html#s4","x":"The four components are combined with weights that sum to one, : where each normalises its component on its support; for , . These are the components asked for in TLO2 and ISA-2 question 1a."},{"t":"Algorithm beam_range_finder_model","c":"Chapter 5","u":"ch5.html#s5","x":"TLO4 asks you to explain the algorithm. Input: . for to : &nbsp;&nbsp;compute by ray casting from the sensor pose in the map &nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp; return m, m, m, /m, weights 0.70 / 0.15 / 0.05 / 0.10. m: , , ; . m (a person in front): , ; . m (max reading): (point mass treated per unit bin). m (behind the wall): only survives, ."},{"t":"Learning the intrinsic parameters from data","c":"Chapter 5","u":"ch5.html#s6","x":"ISA-2 question 2c asks for the algorithm that learns . It is expectation maximisation over a data set of readings with known true ranges : E-step : for every reading compute using the current parameters, and responsibilities , and similarly for short, max and rand. M-step : for each component ; ; . Repeat until the parameters converge."},{"t":"Drawbacks of the beam model","c":"Chapter 5","u":"ch5.html#s7","x":"Review question 1 and ISA-2 question 2a ask for the major drawbacks. Write these five: Lack of smoothness : in cluttered places a small change of pose changes abruptly (a beam that just misses a table leg), so is spiky and particle filters need many particles. Computational cost : every beam of every particle needs a ray cast, typically cells. Precomputing over a pose grid helps but costs memory. "},{"t":"Score the endpoint, not the ray","c":"Chapter 5","u":"ch5.html#s8","x":"The likelihood field (end-point) model gives up the physical ray-casting explanation. It projects the end point of each beam into the map and scores it by the distance from that end point to the nearest obstacle: where is the sensor mounting pose on the robot. The beam likelihood is a mixture of a zero-centred Gaussian on the distance and a uniform term: and max-range readings are simply discarded"},{"t":"What the likelihood field looks like","c":"Chapter 5","u":"ch5.html#s9","x":"The model has three components compared with the beam model's four: noise ( on the distance), failures (max readings ignored) and random measurements. It does not model unexpected short readings explicitly; a person in front of a wall simply produces an endpoint far from any map obstacle, which the uniform term absorbs. Move the pose error slider through 1 m in x. The log likelihood changes smooth"},{"t":"Worked example: scoring a scan with the likelihood field","c":"Chapter 5","u":"ch5.html#s10","x":"m, , , m. The distance lookups for three endpoints are 0.05, 0.12 and 0.60 m. Beam 1: . Beam 2: . Beam 3: , almost only the random floor. ; . Without the random term, beam 3 would contribute , and a beam 1 m from any obstacle would contribute only and wipe out the product: the uniform floor makes the model robust to one bad beam."},{"t":"Use cases and problems of the likelihood field","c":"Chapter 5","u":"ch5.html#s11","x":"Use cases. The likelihood field is the default model of AMCL in ROS and Nav2 ( laser_model_type: likelihood_field ), and the same idea underlies scan matchers in Cartographer and Hector SLAM, which maximise the sum of a smoothed occupancy value at scan endpoints. Criterion Beam model Likelihood field Physical basis Ray casting through free space None; endpoint heuristic Smoothness in pose Poor in "},{"t":"Matching maps instead of beams","c":"Chapter 5","u":"ch5.html#s12","x":"Correlation-based measurement models (map matching) turn the scan into a small local map , an occupancy grid in the robot frame built from the current (or last few) scans, and compare it with the global map at the hypothesised pose. The standard similarity is the correlation coefficient over the overlapping cells: with the average map value and the local map transformed into global coordinates by "},{"t":"Map matching in action","c":"Chapter 5","u":"ch5.html#s13","x":"Correlative scan matching (Olson, 2009) searches a 3D window of exhaustively with multi-resolution grids and is used in Google Cartographer for loop-closure detection and in many industrial AMR localisers that align a scan to a reference map at start-up. Aerial and underwater robots correlate terrain elevation or sonar images the same way."},{"t":"Problems with correlation-based models","c":"Chapter 5","u":"ch5.html#s14","x":"No physical noise model : the correlation is a heuristic similarity; it does not model unexpected objects or failures, so crowds degrade it. Free space counts equally with obstacles: large empty areas correlate strongly everywhere, flattening the score in open halls. Cost : building the local map and comparing many cells per pose hypothesis is more expensive than a likelihood-field lookup, though "},{"t":"Extract features, then model the features","c":"Chapter 5","u":"ch5.html#s15","x":"Feature-based models first reduce the raw measurement to a small set of features, , for example lines, corners, poles, reflectors or fiducial markers. For landmarks sensed by range and bearing, each feature is : range, bearing and signature (colour, reflectivity, ID). Review question 5 and ISA-2 question 2b ask you to explain this model. Features drastically reduce computation (tens of features in"},{"t":"The landmark measurement model","c":"Chapter 5","u":"ch5.html#s16","x":"If feature corresponds to landmark at with signature , the measurement is With known correspondence , the algorithm landmark_model_known_correspondence computes from the pose and the map, and returns , with the three noise terms assumed independent."},{"t":"Worked example: predicting a landmark measurement","c":"Chapter 5","u":"ch5.html#s17","x":"Robot at m heading 20 degrees; landmark at m. m. . If the sensor reports m, with m, , the normalised errors are 0.49 and 0.23 standard deviations: a very plausible match."},{"t":"Sampling poses from a landmark measurement","c":"Chapter 5","u":"ch5.html#s18","x":"Review question 6 asks you to discuss the algorithm sample_landmark_model_known_correspondence. It inverts the measurement: given one landmark observation, it draws poses that could have produced it. Global localization and particle-filter recovery use it to place new particles where the sensor says the robot could be. (the direction from the landmark to the robot is unknown) , return"},{"t":"Use cases and problems of feature-based models","c":"Chapter 5","u":"ch5.html#s19","x":"Fiducial markers (AprilTag, ArUco) on floors and walls give landmarks with unique signatures, so correspondence is trivial; many AGV and AMR products and drone landing pads use them. Reflector-based laser navigation, used on industrial AGVs for decades, measures range and bearing to retro-reflective targets with millimetre-level repeatability. Visual SLAM systems such as ORB-SLAM use image corner "},{"t":"Localization: estimating pose relative to a given map","c":"Chapter 6","u":"ch6.html#s1","x":"Mobile robot localization (position estimation) is the problem of determining the pose of a robot relative to a given map. It is the most basic perceptual problem in mobile robotics: nearly every task, from fetching a tote to docking on a charger, needs the pose. The pose cannot be sensed directly; it must be inferred from data, so localization is a coordinate transformation problem between the ma"},{"t":"Three dimensions that characterise localization problems","c":"Chapter 6","u":"ch6.html#s2","x":"Review question 1 (ESA question 7a) asks for the dimensions that characterise localization problems. Thrun et al. describe the following; the fourth is often added: Local versus global (the initial knowledge and knowledge available at run time): position tracking, global localization, kidnapped robot. Static versus dynamic environments : whether objects other than the robot change state. Dynamic c"},{"t":"Local versus global localization","c":"Chapter 6","u":"ch6.html#s3","x":"TLO2 and review question 2 ask you to distinguish these (ESA question 7b): Criterion Position tracking (local) Global localization Kidnapped robot Initial pose Known approximately Unknown Believed known but wrong Uncertainty Small, local Large, whole map Large, not represented Belief shape Unimodal is enough Must be multimodal Must detect failure and re-globalise Suitable filter EKF, UKF Grid, MCL"},{"t":"Markov localization: the Bayes filter with a map","c":"Chapter 6","u":"ch6.html#s4","x":"Markov localization is the direct application of the Bayes filter to localization. Review question 3 and ESA question 7c ask you to discuss it. Algorithm Markov_localization : for all do It differs from the plain Bayes filter only by conditioning on the map . The initial belief encodes the problem type: a narrow Gaussian around the known start for position tracking; a uniform over the whole map fo"},{"t":"Markov localization in a corridor","c":"Chapter 6","u":"ch6.html#s5","x":"Cells 0-4, doors at 0 and 3; , ; uniform prior 0.2. Sense door: unnormalised (0.12, 0.04, 0.04, 0.12, 0.04); normalised (0.333, 0.111, 0.111, 0.333, 0.111). Move one cell right (exact with 0.8, under- or overshoot 0.1 each, cyclic): (0.156, 0.289, 0.133, 0.133, 0.289). Sense door again: (0.296, 0.183, 0.085, 0.254, 0.183): cell 0 now leads, because cell 4 wrapped to it."},{"t":"EKF localization with known correspondences","c":"Chapter 6","u":"ch6.html#s6","x":"EKF localization implements Markov localization with a Gaussian belief , a feature map and the landmark model of Chapter 5. Review question 4 asks you to explain the algorithm. With the velocity motion model and range-bearing measurements: Prediction : , , where is the motion Jacobian with respect to the pose, with respect to the controls and the control-noise covariance. For every observed featur"},{"t":"Worked EKF localization update","c":"Chapter 6","u":"ch6.html#s7","x":"Prior , ; landmark at ; ; measured . , : . ; . Innovation ; Mahalanobis squared distance 0.40, well inside a validation gate. Posterior mean : the robot is moved diagonally away from the landmark and turned left so the landmark appears at a smaller bearing. Posterior standard deviations m, m, : heading uncertainty shrinks the most, because bearing is the precise measurement."},{"t":"Which landmark did I see?","c":"Chapter 6","u":"ch6.html#s8","x":"Feature-based EKF localization needs the correspondence : which map landmark generated feature . When landmarks have unique signatures (AprilTags) it is known; otherwise it must be estimated. The standard approach is maximum likelihood data association : implemented per feature by computing, for every candidate landmark , the predicted measurement , the innovation covariance , and choosing the wit"},{"t":"When the gates overlap","c":"Chapter 6","u":"ch6.html#s9","x":"An EKF that commits to a wrong correspondence performs a confident update in the wrong direction, shrinks its covariance and then rejects the correct associations that follow. The filter diverges without knowing it. Remedies: use signatures, require mutual consistency of several features (joint compatibility branch and bound), delay the decision, or keep several hypotheses (next topic)."},{"t":"Keep several Gaussians alive","c":"Chapter 6","u":"ch6.html#s10","x":"Multi-hypothesis tracking (MHT) extends EKF localization to multimodal beliefs by representing the belief as a mixture of Gaussians: Each component is an EKF that corresponds to one sequence of data-association decisions, and is its weight, updated by the likelihood of that component's measurements. When a feature could belong to two landmarks, the filter branches into two tracks instead of choosi"},{"t":"Managing hypotheses","c":"Chapter 6","u":"ch6.html#s11","x":"The number of association histories grows exponentially, so MHT must prune. Practical rules: Delete components whose weight falls below a threshold (for example of the largest). Merge components whose means are close in Mahalanobis distance. Cap the number of components. MHT solves global localization when the environment has few, distinctive features and the number of plausible places is small. I"},{"t":"Grid localization: a histogram filter over pose","c":"Chapter 6","u":"ch6.html#s12","x":"Grid localization applies the histogram filter to pose space. The belief is a collection of discrete probabilities over a grid in . Each cycle predicts every cell with the motion model (a convolution) and multiplies by the measurement model evaluated at the cell centre. It solves global localization and the kidnapped robot problem because any belief shape is representable. A 10 m x 6 m room at 5 c"},{"t":"Monte Carlo localization","c":"Chapter 6","u":"ch6.html#s13","x":"Monte Carlo localization (MCL) is the particle filter applied to localization. Each particle is a pose hypothesis. Algorithm MCL : For : ; . Resample particles with probability proportional to ."},{"t":"Making MCL robust: random particles and adaptive sample size","c":"Chapter 6","u":"ch6.html#s14","x":"Plain MCL cannot recover from failure: once all particles have collapsed near a wrong pose, no particle exists near the truth. Augmented MCL injects random particles with probability , where and are long- and short-term averages of the measurement likelihood. When measurements suddenly fit badly (the robot was kidnapped), drops below and random particles appear. KLD sampling adapts the number of p"},{"t":"Mapping with known poses","c":"Chapter 7","u":"ch7.html#s1","x":"Chapters 5 and 6 assumed a map. Occupancy grid mapping builds one, assuming the robot poses are known (from ground truth, a motion-capture system, or a good localizer). The map is a fine grid of binary random variables , each true if cell is occupied, and the mapping problem is to compute A 100 m x 100 m floor at 5 cm has 4 million cells, so possible maps: the joint posterior is intractable. The o"},{"t":"The log-odds form: updates become additions","c":"Chapter 7","u":"ch7.html#s2","x":"Each cell is a static binary state, so its Bayes filter is the binary Bayes filter. Written in log odds , , the update is a simple addition. TLO1 asks you to discuss the occupancy grid mapping algorithm: Algorithm occupancy_grid_mapping : for all cells : if is in the perceptual field of : else: with the prior , zero for a prior of 0.5. The probability is recovered by . Log odds avoid numerical pro"},{"t":"The inverse sensor model for range finders","c":"Chapter 7","u":"ch7.html#s3","x":"TLO2 asks you to discuss the inverse measurement model for range finders. It is called inverse because it reasons from effect to cause: rather than . For a cell at range and bearing from the sensor, find the beam closest in bearing, then: If or : the cell is beyond the reading or outside the beam, return (no information). If and : the beam ended in this cell, return . If : the beam passed through,"},{"t":"Worked log-odds updates and a map being built","c":"Chapter 7","u":"ch7.html#s4","x":"; ; . One hit: , . Two hits: , . Three hits: , . Five hits: . One hit followed by one pass-through returns to 0 and to 0.5: the cell is uncertain again. With clamping at (probabilities 0.029 and 0.971), a cell occupied for an hour can still become free after five consistent pass-throughs, which lets the map follow a moved pallet."},{"t":"Use cases and problems of occupancy grids","c":"Chapter 7","u":"ch7.html#s5","x":"Occupancy grids are the map format of ROS ( nav_msgs/OccupancyGrid , saved as PGM plus YAML), of Nav2 costmaps and of most robot vacuums. They are used for localization (Chapter 6), planning (Chapter 2) and exploration, where the frontier between known free and unknown cells defines where to go next. OctoMap extends them to 3D with octrees for drones and manipulators. Known poses are assumed ; wit"},{"t":"SLAM: a chicken-and-egg problem","c":"Chapter 7","u":"ch7.html#s6","x":"Simultaneous localization and mapping (SLAM) arises when the robot has neither a map nor its pose. Localization needs a map; mapping needs the poses. SLAM estimates both together from controls and measurements alone. There are two forms: Online SLAM is obtained from full SLAM by integrating out past poses. SLAM also has a discrete part, the correspondence between observed features and map features"},{"t":"EKF SLAM: augment the state with the landmarks","c":"Chapter 7","u":"ch7.html#s7","x":"Review question 1 asks you to discuss the EKF algorithm for SLAM. EKF SLAM applies the EKF to online SLAM with a feature map and maximum-likelihood data association. The state vector stacks the pose and all landmark positions: with a covariance. Landmarks are static, so the motion model moves only the pose part. Measurements are range-bearing observations exactly as in EKF localization, except tha"},{"t":"Deriving the EKF SLAM algorithm","c":"Chapter 7","u":"ch7.html#s8","x":"Review question 2 and ESA question 8c ask you to derive the algorithm with known correspondences. Let be the matrix that selects the pose, . Prediction. , where is the velocity-model pose increment . , where is the derivative of with respect to the pose (non-zero only in the column). . Correction for each observed feature with correspondence : if landmark is new, initialise . , , . is the matrix s"},{"t":"Approximations and limiting assumptions of SLAM","c":"Chapter 7","u":"ch7.html#s9","x":"TLO3 and ESA question 8a ask for the approximations and limiting assumptions. For EKF SLAM write these: Gaussian noise and linearisation : motion and measurement models are linearised at the current estimate; large heading uncertainty makes the linearisation poor and the filter overconfident (inconsistent). Feature-based maps with point landmarks; the environment must contain distinct, extractable"},{"t":"Graph SLAM and the loop closure","c":"Chapter 7","u":"ch7.html#s10","x":"Graph-based SLAM solves full SLAM. Each pose is a node; each odometry reading is an edge constraining two consecutive nodes; each re-observation of a known place adds a loop-closure edge between distant nodes. The map is recovered by finding the configuration of nodes that minimises the sum of squared constraint errors weighted by their information matrices: The information matrix of this problem "},{"t":"SLAM in practice","c":"Chapter 7","u":"ch7.html#s11","x":"Robot vacuums from iRobot, Roborock and others run LiDAR or visual SLAM on the device to map a home and clean room by room. ROS 2 users typically map with slam_toolbox (a graph-based 2D LiDAR SLAM) and then localize with AMCL on the saved map. Google Cartographer combines local scan matching into submaps with global loop closure. Warehouse integrators map a site once with SLAM, then run localizati"},{"t":"SLAM with colour and depth cameras","c":"Chapter 7","u":"ch7.html#s12","x":"An RGB-D camera (Microsoft Kinect, Intel RealSense, Orbbec) provides a colour image and a per-pixel depth image, typically 640 x 480 at 30 Hz with useful range of about 0.3 to 5 m. RGB-D SLAM exploits both: visual features give distinctive data association (which LiDAR lacks), and depth gives metric 3D positions (which a monocular camera lacks). Feature extraction : ORB, SIFT or SURF keypoints and"},{"t":"Strengths, limits and systems of RGB-D SLAM","c":"Chapter 7","u":"ch7.html#s13","x":"Criterion 2D LiDAR SLAM RGB-D SLAM Map 2D occupancy grid 3D colour point cloud or voxels Range 10-30 m 0.3-5 m Lighting Independent Colour features need light; depth fails in sunlight (structured light) Data association Geometric only Visual descriptors: strong place recognition Cost Higher sensor cost Low sensor cost, higher compute RTAB-Map (widely used in ROS), ORB-SLAM3 in RGB-D mode and Elast"},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Distinguish between a stationary robot and a mobile robot in terms of construction, workspace, position knowledge and applications. Why does mobility turn a geometry problem into an estimation problem?"},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"List and explain the challenges of mobile autonomous robots. For each challenge give one example and name the technique that addresses it."},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Define mobility, locomotion, holonomy and degrees of freedom for a mobile robot. Explain with the example of a car why a nonholonomic robot can still reach every pose in the plane."},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Explain static and dynamic stability with the support polygon. Classify a car, a Segway, a three-wheeled differential robot with a castor and a hexapod walking with a tripod gait, and justify each."},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Discuss the fundamental requirements of a mobile robot. Explain how frames of reference (world, odometry, robot, sensor) are used to meet the localization requirement in a ROS-based robot."},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Frame {B} is rotated 30 degrees about z relative to {A} and its origin lies at (3, 2) m in {A}. (a) Write the 3 x 3 planar homogeneous transform from {B} to {A}. (b) A point is at (1.5, -0.5) m in {B}; find it in {A}. (c) Write the inverse transform and verify it returns the point."},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Two frames share an origin; {B} is rotated by alpha = 40 degrees about z. Derive the rotation matrix from {B} to {A} and verify numerically that each entry equals the dot product of the corresponding basis vectors. Show that the matrix is orthonormal."},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"A differential-drive robot has wheel radius 0.04 m and wheel separation 0.25 m. The right wheel turns at 15 rad/s and the left at 10 rad/s for 3 s from pose (0, 0, 0). Compute v, omega, the radius of curvature and the final pose."},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"For a differential-drive robot with r = 0.05 m and l = 0.40 m, derive the inverse kinematics and compute the wheel speeds needed for v = 0.6 m/s and omega = -0.8 rad/s. If each wheel is limited to 14 rad/s, what is the largest v that can be combined with this omega?"},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Using feedback inverse kinematics with k_rho = 0.5, k_alpha = 2.0 and k_beta = -0.6, compute rho, alpha, beta and the commands v and omega for a robot at (4, 1) m heading 0 degrees whose goal is (7, 5) m heading 90 degrees. State the stability conditions and check them."},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Compare occupancy grids, topological graphs and continuous (polygon) maps as representations for path planning. Which planner suits each, and what are their weaknesses?"},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Explain the path-planning algorithms Dijkstra, A*, D* and RRT. State for each whether it is complete and optimal, and the kind of problem it suits."},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Discuss variations of the path-planning problem and planning at different length scales. Illustrate with the layers of a ROS 2 Nav2 navigation stack."},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Explain the factors that lead to robot uncertainty and why probabilistic robotics represents uncertainty explicitly. Give two advantages and one cost of the probabilistic approach."},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"What practical runtime concerns, besides asymptotic complexity, arise in sampling-based motion planning? Suggest how to deal with each, and explain path smoothing with its risk."},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Assuming a kd-tree is used as the nearest-neighbour structure and points are sampled uniformly at random, derive the expected run-time of inserting a point into a tree of size N and of building an RRT of N nodes. Compare with a linear scan for N = 10 000 in terms of approximate operation counts."},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"On a 4-connected grid with unit step cost, the start is (0,0), the goal is (4,3) and cells (2,0), (2,1) and (2,2) are blocked. Using the Manhattan heuristic, compute f = g + h for the start, for (1,0), (1,1) and for (1,3), give the optimal path cost, and state why the heuristic is admissible."},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"A robot drives straight along x at 0.5 m/s, updated every 1 s. Speed noise has sd 0.03 m/s and turn-rate noise sd 3 deg/s. Starting with zero covariance, apply Sigma' = G Sigma G^T + V M V^T for 10 steps and give the standard deviations in x, y and theta. Explain why sigma_y exceeds sigma_x."},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"A robot's obstacle detector has p(hit | occupied) = 0.85 and p(hit | free) = 0.15. The prior probability that a cell is occupied is 0.3. Compute the posterior after one hit, after two independent hits, and after a hit followed by a miss."},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Two independent Gaussian estimates of a robot's x-position are N(2.0, 0.8^2) m from odometry and N(3.0, 0.5^2) m from a beacon. Compute the mean and standard deviation of their normalised product, and the probability, under the fused estimate, that the robot is beyond 3.0 m."},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Discuss the typical state variables of a robot interacting with its environment. Define measurement data and control data, state the Markov assumption and draw the dynamic Bayes network."},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Describe the discrete Bayes filter and discuss how the Bayes filter is used in continuous state spaces. Derive the prediction and correction steps from Bayes' rule and total probability."},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Explain the Kalman filter algorithm with neat diagrams. State the linear-Gaussian assumptions and interpret the Kalman gain in the limits of a perfect sensor and a perfect prediction."},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Compare the Kalman filter, extended Kalman filter and information filter. Explain why the EKF may diverge and how the UKF addresses this."},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Explain the histogram filter and the particle filter, including resampling, particle deprivation and the low-variance sampler. Compare them with Gaussian filters in a table."},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"A door-state Bayes filter uses p(sense open | open) = 0.7, p(sense open | closed) = 0.3. Pushing opens a closed door with probability 0.9 and never closes an open one. From bel(open) = 0.5 compute the belief after: do nothing, sense open; push, sense open; do nothing, sense closed."},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"A 1D robot has prior N(10, 2^2) m. It measures z = 12 m with sd 1 m, then moves u = 3 m with motion sd 0.8 m, then measures z = 16 m with sd 1 m. Run the Kalman filter and give the gain, mean and standard deviation after each step."},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Derive the measurement update of the information filter and apply it: prior N(10, 2^2), measurement z = 12 with variance 1 (C = 1). Compute Omega and xi before and after, recover the mean and variance, and compare with the Kalman result."},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"A state x ~ N(0.5, 0.3^2) passes through y = x^2. Compute the EKF-linearised mean and standard deviation of y. Compute the exact mean and variance of y analytically, and comment on the linearisation error."},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Five particles are at x = 0.5, 1.5, 2.5, 3.5, 4.5 m. A range sensor with Gaussian noise sd 0.6 m reports the robot at 3.1 m. Compute normalised importance weights, the weighted mean, and the effective sample size. With the low-variance sampler and r = 0.1, which particles are selected?"},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Explain probabilistic kinematics. Illustrate the kinematic configuration of a rigid mobile robot in a planar environment and distinguish the density and sampling forms of a motion model."},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Discuss the velocity motion model: its assumptions, the role of each noise parameter alpha1 to alpha6, why the extra rotation gamma is required, and its applications."},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Explain the algorithm sample_motion_model_velocity line by line and describe how it is used in a particle filter. What must be done when the sampled omega is close to zero?"},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Discuss the odometry motion model algorithm. Why is relative odometry used instead of absolute odometry, and what does each of alpha1 to alpha4 represent?"},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Distinguish between the velocity and odometry motion models, and explain the banana-shaped distribution and the practical problems of tuning motion noise, including for a mobile manipulator."},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"A robot at (0, 0, 0) executes v = 0.8 m/s and omega = 0.4 rad/s for 2.5 s. Compute the centre and radius of the circular arc and the noise-free final pose using the velocity motion model."},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Using motion_model_velocity, compute p(x_t | u_t, x_t-1) for x_t-1 = (0, 0, 0), u = (1.0 m/s, 0.5 rad/s), dt = 2 s, hypothesis x_t = (1.70 m, 1.10 m, 62 deg), alpha = (0.01, 0.005, 0.01, 0.02, 0.002, 0.002). Show mu, the circle centre, r*, v-hat, omega-hat, gamma-hat and the three densities."},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Odometry reports a move from (1.0, 2.0, 10 deg) to (3.5, 3.0, 55 deg). Compute delta_rot1, delta_trans and delta_rot2. Apply the noise-free relative motion to a particle at (5.0, 1.0, 90 deg) and give its new pose."},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"For the odometry reading of the previous question with alpha = (0.02, 0.005, 0.01, 0.002), compute the standard deviations used to sample delta_rot1, delta_trans and delta_rot2. Estimate the 1-sigma lateral spread of the sampled end positions."},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"A mobile manipulator base is at (4.0 m, 2.0 m, 30 deg). A planar 2-link arm (0.5 m, 0.4 m) is mounted 0.2 m ahead of the base centre with joint angles 20 deg and 40 deg. Compute the end-effector position in the world frame. If the base heading has sd 2 deg, estimate the resulting sd of end-effector position."},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Explain maps (feature-based and location-based) and the components of the beam model of range finders, with sketches of each component and the mixture."},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Discuss the major drawbacks and the sources of noise and uncertainty of the beam-based sensor model."},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Discuss the algorithm for learning the intrinsic parameters of the beam-based sensor model from data."},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Explain the likelihood field and correlation-based sensor models and compare them with the beam model. Discuss use cases and problems of each."},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Explain feature-based sensor models, including feature extraction, the landmark measurement model with signatures, and the sampling of poses from a landmark measurement."},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"A beam has expected range z* = 4 m, z_max = 8 m, sigma_hit = 0.2 m, lambda_short = 0.5 /m and weights 0.75 / 0.10 / 0.05 / 0.10. Compute p(z | x, m) for readings 4.1 m, 2.0 m and 8.0 m, and the product for the three beams."},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"In a likelihood-field model with sigma_hit = 0.25 m, z_hit = 0.9, z_rand = 0.1 and z_max = 10 m, four beam endpoints lie 0.02, 0.10, 0.35 and 0.80 m from the nearest obstacle. Compute each beam likelihood, the scan likelihood and its logarithm. Which beam would you suspect and why?"},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"A robot at (2.0, 1.0, 45 deg) observes a landmark at (6.0, 4.0) m. Compute the expected range and bearing. The sensor reports 5.1 m and -7.0 deg with sigma_r = 0.15 m and sigma_phi = 2.5 deg. Compute the landmark_model_known_correspondence likelihood (ignore the signature)."},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Using sample_landmark_model_known_correspondence for a landmark at (5, 5) m, a measurement r = 3.0 m, phi = 25 deg, and a sampled gamma = 200 deg (take the noise samples as zero), compute the sampled pose. Verify by recomputing the range and bearing from that pose."},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"A 3 x 4 global map patch has occupied cells at (0,1), (0,2), (1,1), (1,2) and all others free (1 = occupied, 0 = free). The local map at a hypothesised pose has occupied cells (0,1), (0,2), (1,1). Using the mean over both maps, compute the correlation coefficient and the likelihood max(rho, 0)."},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Discuss the dimensions that characterise different localization problems, with an example of each."},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Distinguish between local and global localization and the kidnapped robot problem. Which filters can solve each, and why?"},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Discuss the Markov localization algorithm with a one-dimensional corridor example, and explain how the initial belief encodes the localization problem."},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Explain the EKF localization algorithm with known correspondences and describe how correspondences are estimated when unknown. Why are wrong associations dangerous?"},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Compare multi-hypothesis tracking, grid localization and Monte Carlo localization. Explain augmented MCL and KLD sampling."},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"A cyclic corridor has 6 cells with doors in cells 0 and 2. p(door | door) = 0.7, p(door | wall) = 0.1. Starting uniform, the robot senses a door, moves right one cell (succeeds with 0.8, stays with 0.2), and senses a door again. Compute the belief after each step and state the most likely cell."},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"EKF localization: prior mean (1.0, 2.0, 0 deg), covariance diag(0.2^2, 0.2^2, (8 deg)^2); landmark at (4.0, 2.0); Q = diag(0.1^2, (2 deg)^2); measurement (2.9 m, 4 deg). Compute z-hat, H, S, K, the posterior mean and posterior standard deviations."},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"A measured feature at (3.4, 2.2) m must be associated with one of three predicted landmarks A (3.0, 2.0), B (3.7, 2.5), C (4.2, 1.6), each with innovation covariance [[0.08, 0.02], [0.02, 0.05]] m^2. Compute squared Mahalanobis distances, apply a 95 percent gate (5.99), and choose the maximum-likelihood association."},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"An MHT localizer holds three hypotheses with weights 0.5, 0.3 and 0.2. A new measurement has likelihoods 0.01, 0.30 and 0.05 under the three hypotheses. Compute the updated weights. If hypotheses below 5 percent are pruned, which remain and what are their renormalised weights?"},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"A 40 m x 25 m warehouse is localized with a grid filter at 10 cm and 5 deg resolution. Compute the number of cells and memory at 4 bytes per cell. If a 20-beam likelihood-field evaluation costs 1 microsecond, how long does one full update take, and how many MCL particles could be updated in the same time? Comment."},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Discuss the occupancy grid mapping algorithm, including the independence assumption, the log-odds form and its use cases and problems."},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Discuss the inverse measurement model for robots equipped with range finders. Why is it called inverse, and what do alpha and beta represent?"},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Discuss the extended Kalman filter algorithm for the simultaneous localization and mapping problem, including the state vector, prediction, correction and the role of correlations."},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Explain the approximations and limiting assumptions of SLAM, and compare EKF SLAM, FastSLAM and graph SLAM."},{"t":"L2 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"Explain RGB-D SLAM: the sensor, the front-end and back-end pipeline, and its strengths and limitations compared with 2D LiDAR SLAM."},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"A cell starts at p = 0.5. The inverse sensor model gives p_occ = 0.65 for hits and p_free = 0.40 for pass-throughs. Log odds are clamped to +/-2.0. Compute l and p after the sequence hit, hit, free, hit, free, free, free."},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"A beam along a row of 25 cm cells (centres at 0, 0.25, ..., 1.75 m) returns z = 1.5 m with z_max = 4 m, alpha = 0.25 m, p_occ = 0.7, p_free = 0.3. Classify each cell with the inverse sensor model and give its log-odds increment. What are the cell probabilities after two identical scans?"},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"An EKF SLAM system maps 500 point landmarks in 2D. Compute the state dimension, the number of covariance entries and the memory in double precision. By what factor do the time per update and memory grow if the map grows to 2000 landmarks? Suggest two remedies."},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"In EKF SLAM a robot at (0, 0, 0) first observes a new landmark at range 2.5 m and bearing 30 deg. Initialise the landmark estimate, then compute the 2 x 5 low-dimensional Jacobian H for this landmark at the initialised estimate."},{"t":"L3 question","c":"Questions","u":"questions.html","x":"A 1D pose graph has poses x0..x3 with x0 fixed at 0. Odometry edges say x1 - x0 = 1.1, x2 - x1 = 1.0, x3 - x2 = 0.9 m (weight 1). A loop closure says x3 - x0 = 3.2 m with weight 9 (sd one third). Solve the weighted least-squares problem and give the corrected poses. Compare with dead reckoning."}];